WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.282
This query partitions employees by department and orders salary descending.

00:00:04.282 --> 00:00:07.177
RANK restarts at one for each department.

00:00:07.177 --> 00:00:10.557
Run the query and notice the boundaries between departments.

00:00:10.557 --> 00:00:16.722
This is a common pattern for top earners, top products, or highest values within categories.

00:00:16.722 --> 00:00:21.657
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:21.657 --> 00:00:25.772
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:29.957
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:29.957 --> 00:00:34.612
In a real setting such as a hospital ranking visits and identifying readmissions,

00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:40.362
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:40.362 --> 00:00:41.897
before discussing syntax.

00:00:41.897 --> 00:00:47.600
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
